german car maker volkswagen is facing  prosecutors in the country over  allegations of human rights violations  at a farm it ran  during brazil's military dictatorship  accusations include include slave labor  torture and even killings prosecutors  say they have documented years of  atrocities committed by volkswagen  managers in the 1970s and 80s and now  they hope to reach a financial  settlement for the victims  a courthouse in brasilia here behind  closed doors prosecutors were set to  confront one of the world's most  powerful automakers with a series of  explosive allegations it revolves around  this farm in brazil the accusation that  volkswagen benefited from slave labor  here during the 1970s and 80s  my father it was a form of modern  slavery  workers had to work seven days a week  more than 10 hours a day without any pay  the prosecution alleges that there are  hundreds of cases in which serious human  rights violations occurred  they say workers were little more than  indentured servants  witnesses speak of being shackled after  attempting to escape being forced to  work at gunpoint and even of murder  when someone tried to escape the  managers would go after them and shoot  them  they beat up those who tried to escape  on the street in the shacks  volkswagen bought the farm in 1973 at  the invitation of brazil's military  dictatorship  today the company says they take the  accusations seriously but must first  analyze the results of the investigation  before commenting  this surprises christopher copper who  says he informed volkswagen of its  involvement a few years ago  i examined the operations of this farm  during my studies in 2017.  after my discovery i told volkswagen  that during this period it had used  foreign laborers  who were essentially forced into slavery  brazil's prosecutor now wants justice  we are convinced that volkswagen will  acknowledge its responsibility and that  there will be a settlement with the  prosecutor's office and the former  workers brazilian society will be  compensated for these unacceptable  incidents  it's unclear how volkswagen will respond  to the allegations  we asked our correspondent matthias eva  to what extent volkswagen management  knew about what was going on  they knew about it and there were  reports already in the 80s in the german  magazine spiegel about it so there were  rumors about it um  for decades already and but lately in  2017 uh the historian christopher copper  which we heard in the report he  published his report um  and folks like knew about  these  accusations and about the the what  happened at this farm because mr copper  dedicated one chapter uh on this farm in  the para state in brazil so they knew  about it also the management but um yeah  we don't know why they didn't act so far  that's one of the secrets volkswagen  might  might tell us about  in the next months which are to come  you
 

